            <ArticleTitle>Extraosseous, epidural cavernous hemangioma with back pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>206-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.12659/PJR.893424</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Cavernous malformations are characterized by enlarged vascular structures located in benign neural tissues within the cerebellum and spinal cord of the central nervous system. Cavernous hemangiomas (CHs) account for 5% to 12% of all spinal vascular malformations.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">We removed a hemorrhagic thoracic mass in a 40-year-old male patient who presented with progressive neurological deficits.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We found it appropriate to present this case due to its rarity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
